Z\\ge 1) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.1587.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-276365/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276365"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335887/field-1/8745942d11881f34cc952f8daef25fa0d8118abe2bc95976960834caed9f8f6d" :answer/id #uuid "62f53296-d904-5665-b40e-c00401698cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9793" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335887/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "847b2a96-eea6-5868-be71-29779072a33b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335887/field-1/8745942d11881f34cc952f8daef25fa0d8118abe2bc95976960834caed9f8f6d" :answer-field/choices ["q-335887/field-1/8745942d11881f34cc952f8daef25fa0d8118abe2bc95976960834caed9f8f6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335887" :question/id #uuid "58af10d2-2fad-513b-954f-3311d42d9dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335887" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(8,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1.88).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1.88)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =8$ and $\\