$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-5$ and $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.64<X<3.64) & =P(\\frac{0.64-5}{4}<Z<\\frac{3.64-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-1.09<Z<-0.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/6c919bfe71b67ed94751cb53bd5f24e9e80e7979a28a8e28b123aac907d6b7e4.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.09<Z<-0.34) & =P(Z<-0.34)-P(Z\\le -1.09) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.34)-P(Z\\le -1.09) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.34)-Φ(-1.09)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.34) & =0.3669 \\\\ Φ(-1.09) & =0.1379\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.09<Z<-0.34) & =Φ(-0.34)-Φ(-1.09) \\\\  & =0.3669-0.1379 \\\\  & =0.2290.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(0.64<X<3.64)=0.2290.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94318/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94318"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8203"] :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(2,2^2),$ compute $P(X <               1.24).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X <       x) = P \\left( Z <       \\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 2$ and $\\sigma = 2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.24) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.24-2}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.38) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.38) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/48d92806543cb86b2906032b1723fa942db842e7f283681cafeeb9b11fca8cab.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.38) = 0.3520.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X <               1.24) = 0.3520.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8204"] :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y \\sim N(5,4^2),$ compute $P(Y \\geq 2.92).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2743 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3085 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3050 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3015 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq y) = P \\left( Z \\geq \\dfrac{y-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 5$ and $\\sigma = 4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 2.92) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{2.92-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.52)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/049e4733082674668ebbe2c5a710cd847f1db16d26fdcd2af6e603941efdffe7.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.52) & =1-P(Z<-0.52) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.52) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.52)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.52) =0.3015.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.52) & =1-Φ(-0.52) \\\\  & =1-0.3015 \\\\  & =0.6985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq 2.92) = 0.6985.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8205"] :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(1,9),$ compute $P(-1.13 <  X <       3.25).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a <        X <        b) = P \\left( \\dfrac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} <        Z <        \\dfrac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 1$ and $\\sigma = \\sqrt 9 = 3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.13<X<3.25) & =P(\\frac{-1.13-1}{3}<Z<\\frac{3.25-1}{3}) \\\\  & =P(-0.71<Z<0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/a50513f550d8a3122e2f2276883a0189fe9bdc6dd19159e8ae94dbd5b247f5a9.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =P(Z<0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =Φ(0.75)-Φ(-0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.71) & =0.2389 \\\\ Φ(0.75) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =Φ(0.75)-Φ(-0.71) \\\\  & =0.7734-0.2389 \\\\  & =0.5345.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-1.13<          X <          3.25) = 0.5345.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
